the main a2 road into dover the queue  was continuous  the uk government estimated some 1500  lorries had been stranded  but a drive around the approach roads to  the port revealed many many more than  that  parked up and waiting for news news  eventually came  that the blockade would be lifted but  only for those with proof of a negative  covid test  and that is no use at all to the  thousands of drivers  whose hopes of reaching home for  christmas are now gone  now it's done it's finished  from here to go home we have about  almost 2 000 miles it's about  45 hours to drive no stop  no sleep just drive  and we can make it if covet testing is  to be the precondition for reopening the  border with france that presents an  enormous logistical challenge for the uk  authorities  there are now thousands of truck and van  drivers parked up in lay bys  all across the county of kent and  getting the testing to them  or them to the testing is going to be an  enormous task  and all this just days before christmas  this german businessman was just minutes  away from boarding his ferry  when the blockade came into force yes  three cars from freedom so if i'd been  10 minutes earlier i would be now on the  other side  and be safe and at home for christmas  with my family  he's resigned to the possibility that  he'll spend christmas stuck in dover  but he says the uk authorities have been  totally inadequate  down there there are public toilets  which are not open for the drivers  they should be open i think they should  be handled coffee or tea to the drivers  immediately  government should do something and they  should do it immediately  the airfield at manston in kent has been  made a temporary lorry park but the  hundreds of vehicles here are again  just a section of the true scale of the  problem and the scarcity of food and  sanitary facilities  is also creating tensions the member for  parliament for dover  blames france for this crisis i just  think the way in which the french  government reacted  was just really unhelpful i mean the uk  and the french government the haulage  industry the ferry industry  they've worked hand in glove over the  last few months since trans  since the virus started to make sure  that it's managed and i think that's  what should have happened here  as night fell in dover the rain arrived  and the drivers staged a blockade of  their own  [Applause]  [Music]  many of these truckers and van drivers  now facing their third night stranded  here at dover tempers are clearly  starting to fray  they've occupied the roundabout which is  here at the entrance  to the port behind me they've blocked  the traffic there is a long snaking line  of lorries in that direction  the police are trying to restore order  but for the moment  they're not being successful i stayed in  the parking on two days  my my children my son my  daughter is crying for me  reinforcements restored order but the  situation  remains tense paul brennan al jazeera  dover
 

